  "economic_interpretation": [
    "A 1.0 revenue/net-investment case is economically credible: several 3\u20135-year leader windows cluster near 0.93\u20131.09 using narrow trade WC. This improves external support for the existing bull, rather than proving Intel will attain it.",
    "Ending in a trough instead yields roughly 0.50. The denominator contains capacity built for later years; revenue includes node/mix/pricing and FX. These are broad cash-efficiency diagnostics, not calibrated marginal-capital coefficients or probability bounds.",
    "2021\u21922025 total revenue rose about 140%, while 12-inch-equivalent wafer shipments rose only about 6%. Mix, technology value, packaging and pricing therefore matter enormously. The ratio does not prove a physical volume growth rate can be financed at that efficiency.",
    "Intel combines merchant CPU design value and internal manufacturing, giving it potentially greater sales per physical wafer, but also greater competitive, startup, outside-foundry and development burden. TSMC revenue/net capital cannot mechanically set Intel Products turnover.",
    "The earlier single-quarter TSMC annualized sales/average net PP&E 1.4 is a stock ratio and remains inadmissible as direct support for an incremental 1.4 parameter.",
    "Existing Intel 0.8 remains a reasonable owned planning assumption; 1.0 is a credible favorable case, and a fair thesis must retain it. Sources do not establish that 0.8 is the expected value or make 1.0 unlikely.",
    "TSMC discloses customers generally do not submit product purchase orders far in advance, while some pay capacity-retention advances. Forecast judgment can rely on design/customer/equipment readiness and funding terms; requiring signed volume contracts for every future wafer dollar is an unreasonable blanket rule."
  ],
